caption: Teaching and access
engine-rule: teaching-and-access

Anyone who personally knows a Jutsu may teach it to another practitioner of the same Tradition who has access to the same institutional instance and meets every prerequisite. Mastery is not required.

Teaching grants permission to purchase the advantage. It does not grant character points, waive prerequisites, reveal unknown nodes, or transmit other parts of the tree by implication.

A practitioner entering through instruction receives the instructor's existing tree instance. A Class-granted practitioner who has no instructor defines one initial legal Mastery-eligible tree before purchasing anything from the Tradition. That initial institutional tree may later be extended by legal Jutsu authorship; later additions do not create a new instance merely because the tree has grown.

Additional tree instances normally require instruction and may be begun only after the first tree is mastered. A Tradition may explicitly override this rule. Chosen is the reference exception: its Caste, Favored, or comparable access traits may grant uninstructed access to multiple complete Charm trees.
